ArchiveAscii: write both hex digits of a raw byte, not one plus stale memory.

`WriteArchiveAscii::write_raw` hex-encodes each byte with `std::to_chars` and
then decides how many digits it wrote by testing `buf[1] == '\0'`. `to_chars`
does not null-terminate — it reports the digit count through the returned
pointer and touches nothing past it — so `buf[1]` only holds `'\0'` until the
first byte that needs two digits. After that it holds *that* byte's low nibble,
and every subsequent byte below `0x10` is written as `<digit><stale nibble>`
instead of `0<digit>`.

The first sub-0x10 byte of a file therefore survives and every one after it is
corrupt, in every `raw:` entry the ASCII writer produces. Measured on the
LEVEL-VOB of a retail `OldCamp.zen` re-save (docs/engine-acceptance-2026-08-25.md
§10.2, defect A1): `trafoOSToWSPos=vec3:0 0 0` comes back as twelve `05` bytes
— 6.25e-36 per axis — and the identity rotation as `0505803f 0f0f0f0f...`.
The filler is always the previous byte's low nibble.

It is also why ZenKit could not re-load its own ASCII output: the corruption
lands in the packed `zCVob` flag word (`bit1` 0x0002 -> 0x0828 on our fixture's
root VOB, turning on `has_visual_object`), after which `VirtualObject::load`
demands an object frame where the archive holds a plain entry and the read
fails with `type mismatch: expected enum, got: string`.

The fix reads the digit count from `to_chars`'s result and writes the two
characters directly, so the buffer never has to carry a terminator at all.

--- a/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
@@ -373,16 +373,21 @@
 		this->_m_write->write_string(name);
 		this->_m_write->write_string("=raw:");
 
-		std::array<char, 3> buf {};
-		buf[2] = '\0';
+		// `std::to_chars` does not null-terminate: the digit count is only in
+		// the returned pointer. Testing `buf[1] == '\0'` instead left every
+		// byte below 0x10 carrying the previous byte's low nibble as its
+		// second digit, so every raw entry after the first sub-0x10 byte of
+		// the file was corrupt.
+		std::array<char, 2> buf {};
 		for (auto i = 0u; i < length; ++i) {
-			std::to_chars(buf.data(), buf.data() + buf.size(), static_cast<unsigned char>(v[i]), 16);
+			auto const res = std::to_chars(buf.data(), buf.data() + buf.size(), static_cast<unsigned char>(v[i]), 16);
 
-			if (buf[1] == '\0') {
+			if (res.ptr == buf.data() + 1) {
 				this->_m_write->write_char('0');
 				this->_m_write->write_char(buf[0]);
 			} else {
-				this->_m_write->write_string(buf.data());
+				this->_m_write->write_char(buf[0]);
+				this->_m_write->write_char(buf[1]);
 			}
 		}
 
